preConditionHook:
// A climactic-exhaustion profit take: the last climaxBars bars are ALL
// with-trend trend bars and the position is showing an open profit. Brooks
// says explicitly to take profits here and NOT to initiate the opposite
// position, which is why this is an Exit and not an Entry.
//
// The profit gate cannot be supplied by another pane -- exits are ORed, so a
// bare run-of-trend-bars exit would fire in a loss too. Same reason
// NumBarsIfProfitable exists alongside NumBars, and the reason this is not
// StrongTrendBarRun placed in the exit pane (an Entry rule cannot sit there).
// Not ConsecutiveCloseExit either: that counts RISING CLOSES against the prior
// close, with no body test and no profit gate.
//
// All-of-N in the StrongTrendBarRun shape: start true, let a failing bar clear
// the flag, loop unconditionally so EasyLanguage's lack of short-circuiting
// cannot matter. Both flags are class members and are reset at the top.
//
// ctx.OpenPositionProfit() is NET of the entry side's commission, slippage and
// accrued swap (VERIFIED -- one side, not the round turn), which is the
// intended 'actually in profit' reading.
//
// Product, not quotient, for the body test: EL raises a runtime
// divide-by-zero and a zero-range bar is legal data. Deepest bar read is
// climaxBars - 1.
allBull = true;
allBear = true;
for (kk = 0; kk < (int)climaxBars; ++kk) {
    if (el_le(close[kk], open[kk])) allBull = false;
    if (el_lt(close[kk] - open[kk], bodyFraction * (high[kk] - low[kk]))) allBull = false;
    if (el_ge(close[kk], open[kk])) allBear = false;
    if (el_lt(open[kk] - close[kk], bodyFraction * (high[kk] - low[kk]))) allBear = false;
}
